Z||000000||Linc1Tampa||12-09-2001||02:08 PM||||87, 4-cyl, 5-sp<BR>Current condition, car will not run. The injector seems to be spraying an extremely large amount of fuel and flooding the engine.  <BR>Replaced so far: Computer, O2 sensor, MAP sensor, MAT sensor, IAC, PCV, Vacuum lines, EGR, Intake manifold gasket, throttle body gasket, coil packs, crank trigger, spark plugs and wires, fuel pressure regulator, tried a different throttle body, all with no success.<BR>History: Driving home one day the car gave a code 44 and stared stumbling.  Since then the car will not run long enough to get it out of the parking lot and no long gives a code 44.  Checked and I am getting spark, just way too much fuel.  The only other thing I can think of would be to replace the whole wire harness.  If anyone has more ideas or if there is someone that lives in the Tamps, FL area that could look at it I would be grateful.<BR>Lincoln<BR>87 4-cyl (the “Money Pit”)<BR>84 Indy (v6, 5-sp Isuzu, Fast-Back)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||johnt671||12-09-2001||05:29 PM||||If the temp. sensor for the ECM failes, the computer thinks the temp. is -30 degrees and the car will run way rich.||||reg||1
Z||000002||West Coast Fiero||12-09-2001||06:23 PM||||just a quick checkout - check to see that wires in the EGR soleniod ( terminals A and B ) are not linked. This could have been done by mistake from a previous owner trying to delete the EGR system, which really should have been C and D.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||watts||12-09-2001||06:30 PM||||He's got a 4cyl in that car - no EGR solenoid used.<P>Do you get fuel spray from the injector during the 1-2 seconds that the fuel pump runs during initial key-on?<BR>||||reg||1
