Z||000007||fierospeeder||12-10-2001||06:12 AM||||first you can see if the fuel filter is clogged.  one test is taking it out and see if you can blow through it like a straw.  And it should be that easy.   But dont get gas in your mouth.<P><BR>2nd, get a light tester or a volt tester, and see if your getting any juice at the injector while someone is cranking the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-10-2001||08:52 PM||||If you take off a fuel filter, just replace it. Unless it's been replaced recently it likely needs it anyway. It's not worth going to the trouble just to test it.<P>If it's dumping fuel, and you already replaced most of the system.... It is posible there is a short. The injector is fired by grounding it in the ECM. If the wire is damaged it can be locked full on. <P>If you think you've got Low fuel pressure, it can be a couple things. the fuel filter or pump could do it. so could a failed regulator in the TBI.<P>follow the rear fuel line from the TBI until you find a rubber section. CAREFULLY punch the rubber line and see if it starts. If it does then the fuel pressure regulator has quit.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Linc1Tampa||12-14-2001||08:05 PM||||Tonight I unplugged the fuel pump and A/C relays and started my car.  It started right up and had perfect throttle response with no stumble.  The car idled for a little while and gave a code 45.  While it was idling I plugged the a/c and fuel pump relays back in, I tapped the gas, and the injector shot a flood of fuel into the engine, the engine stumbled and died. Does anyone have any suggestions. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||JetroGT||12-15-2001||08:16 AM||||Bad TPS sensor? Mabie it thinks the throttle is wide open.<BR>If I rember right it's an expensive part. $70. Check it with the multi-meter. Two of the pins will be the resistor and the other will be the wiper. Sorry, I don't know the values.<p>[This message has been edited by JetroGT (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-15-2001||02:09 PM||||A bad FP relay could be drawing too much power from the ECM.... In such a case the ECM power supply may be overloading and that could cause all sorts of wierd ass problems.<P>try pluggin in only one or the other. <P>I didn't think the AC relay was used in any way by the ECM, but I haven't read over that circuit in awhile...<P>The AC clutch connector on the harness has a diode in it to kill spikes from the AC clutch coil when it shuts off. If that diode is bad then the AC may be spiking the car enough to crash the ECM. If you need it, MotorMite makes a replacement peice with a new diode that simply plugs in between the car and the AC clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||12-15-2001||04:13 PM||||Ac relay is used by the ECM acording to the wiring I just reread....<P>again... try one at a time. that or just replace both. they aren't very expencive. The ones you have are likely the originals.<P>Also, physically follow the wires as far as you can just to make sure they aren't cooked or rubbed someplace.<P>To get the relays off the bracket... you take the bracket off the firewall and pry up a tab that locks the relay onto it. If you have something long enough and the right thickness it might slide between the bracket and relay from the top but I usually find it faster to take them off the firewall...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||GTDude||12-15-2001||09:34 PM||||The ecm does not send power to the injector as previously mentioned.  A red wire from the fuse box directly powers the injector.  A blue wire that comes from the ecm sends a ground pulse to the injector.  So the longer the ground pulse from the ecm.....the more fuel sprayed. <P>I believe you have a blue wire that is shorted to ground.  To find out...unplug the ecm wiring and the connector at the injector.  Check with an ohm meter...the blue wire at the injector to a good ground.  If it shows any continuity, then this is definitely your problem.  You will then need to trace the wiring (blue wire) from the injector to the  ecm.<P>I had this same problem on a 6000 and it almost drove me crazy. Hope this helps.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||12-15-2001||11:20 PM||||He said the car runs fine except when the thing has the AC and FP relays plugged in. That eliminates the Injector being a problem... <P>The FP relay coil is driven by the ECM's power.<BR>The AC Relay is on when it grounds thru the ECM's driver.<P>Of the 2 I suspect the FP relay more but either could cause trouble.<P>The code 45 could be a bad MAT sensor or it was flooded. Those will sometimes throw a 45 durring idle if they are bad. read DIS notes in my cave... Reset the ECM. If it returns then the MAT is probably flakey<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
